<SiteLayout title="WebP vs AVIF for the Web | ConvertAnyFile" description="Compare WebP and AVIF for modern image delivery, including compression, compatibility, decode cost, quality, and fallback strategy.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>WebP vs AVIF</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Modern web formats</span><h1>WebP vs AVIF: test the whole delivery workflow.</h1><p>Both formats can be excellent on the web. The better choice is the one that delivers the visual quality, file size, decode behavior, and compatibility your actual audience needs.</p></div></section>
<section class="article-shell"><article class="site-container article"><h2>Why both formats exist</h2><p>Traditional JPG and PNG remain useful, but modern websites often want more compression efficiency. WebP and AVIF were created to provide more flexible or efficient image delivery while supporting features such as transparency that are not available in ordinary JPEG.</p><h2>WebP is a practical default for many sites</h2><p>WebP has mature browser support and can represent both lossy and lossless images. It is widely used for modern website delivery because it combines useful compression with broad compatibility in current browsers.</p><h2>AVIF can compress very efficiently</h2><p>AVIF can produce impressive results at small file sizes for some images. That does not mean every AVIF file will beat every WebP file. Encoder settings, source content, image dimensions, and the quality target all matter.</p><h2>Do not compare quality numbers across codecs</h2><p>A quality value of 80 in one encoder does not mean the same thing as 80 in another. Treat the slider as an encoder control, not a universal percentage of truth. Compare the rendered result and the resulting bytes.</p><h2>Dimensions often matter more than format</h2><p>A 5000-pixel-wide image displayed at 800 pixels is still wasteful even if it is encoded as AVIF. Resize for the intended display size before chasing tiny differences between codecs.</p><h2>Use fallbacks when the audience requires them</h2><p>A common web strategy is to serve a modern format to capable browsers while keeping a compatible fallback for environments that need it. The exact strategy depends on the browsers and platforms your product supports.</p><div class="callout"><strong>What to test</strong><p>Compare visual quality at real display size, transfer bytes, decode performance on slower devices, and compatibility with your CMS or image pipeline. The newest format is not automatically the best operational choice.</p></div></article></section>
</SiteLayout>
